<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Hormozdgan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/224">224 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Hormozdgan was fought in 224 CE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> and the Parthian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artabanus-iv">Artabanus IV</a>. Artabanus was killed, and Ardashir was soon crowned king of kings. The battle ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hormozdgan.jpg" alt="Battle of Hormozdgan" width="320" height="132" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eugène Flandin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ardachir_relief_Firuzabad_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Around 208 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vologases-vi">Vologases VI</a> succeeded his father Vologases V as king of the Arsacid Empire, but from 213 he fought a dynastic struggle with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artabanus-iv">Artabanus IV</a>. By 216 Artabanus held most of the empire and was acknowledged as supreme ruler by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. He contained the forces of the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a> in 217, and the peace of the following year left the Arsacids most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, though Vologases went on minting coins and challenging him.</p><p>Meanwhile the Sasanian family had risen to prominence in its native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pars">Pars</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> it began conquering neighbouring regions and more distant lands such as Kirman. Artabanus at first paid little heed, and only later chose to confront him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The site of the battle has not been found. The Arabic chronicle Nihayat al-arab names a place that has been read as Golpayegan, which is improbable, while Bal'ami placed it at Khosh-Hormoz, another name for Ram-Hormoz near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>, on a plain well suited to cavalry. According to al-Tabari, whose work probably drew on Sasanian sources, the two kings agreed to meet at Hormozdgan at the end of the month of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mihr</a>.</p><p>Ardashir arrived early, took an advantageous position on the plain, dug a ditch to protect his forces and seized a spring. He had 10,000 cavalry, some in flexible chain armour like that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>; Artabanus led more men, hampered by heavier lamellar armour. Ardashir's son and heir Shapur also fought. The battle was fought on 28 April 224, and Artabanus was defeated and killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ardashir executed Dad-windad, the dead king's chief secretary, took the title shahanshah and began the conquest of the lands that would be called Iranshahr. At Ardashir-Khwarrah, present-day Firuzabad, he had two rock reliefs carved: one of combats, with Shapur impaling Dad-windad with his lance and Ardashir ousting Artabanus, the other of Ardashir receiving the badge of kingship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vologases-vi">Vologases VI</a> was driven out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> soon after 228. The great Parthian noble families, the Seven Great Houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, kept their power under their new Sasanian overlords and supplied much of their cavalry, so that the state has been called the empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. Attempts to restore Arsacid rule by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-chobin">Bahram Chobin</a> and Vistahm in the late 6th century failed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Hormozdgan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ardashir took advantage of Parthian weakness and defeated and killed Artabanus IV at the <b>Battle of Hormozdgan</b> in 224 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sasanian Empire was founded in 224 CE when Ardashir I, a local ruler from Pars (modern Fars in south-western Iran) and a vassal of the Arsacid Parthian dynasty, defeated and killed the last Parthian king, Artabanus IV, at the <b>Battle of Hormozdgan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
